{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"where-predicate-publication-and-joined-result-key-oracle-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WHERE predicate publication and joined result key oracle review"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reviewed-state-and-claim","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed state and claim"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"49dc79d4"},{"type":"text","value":", the head of pull request #1956 before this review. This\nrecord reviews the Git tree that contains it. The final commit or pull request\nidentifies that immutable tree. The review applied the ORC-001 through ORC-014\nrequirements of the oracle guide revision that adds ORC-013 and ORC-014."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The WHERE predicate publication oracle claims that a filtered live-query\nCollection, direct subscriptions with and without initial state, peer\nsubscriptions routed by one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":" field, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"currentStateAsChanges"},{"type":"text","value":" publish\nexactly the rows whose predicate is TRUE under SQL three-valued logic. The\nclaim covers the grammar in the oracle's opening prose. It does not cover\ncomparison operators other than "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":", joins, ordering, optimistic updates or\ndeletes, truncate, failed replay, or generated cleanup and restart histories."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The joined result key oracle claims that an inner, left, or full two-source\njoin publishes one row per joined pair and that distinct pairs never share a\nresult key, for string keys, numeric keys, both infinities, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":". It does\nnot cover joins over subqueries, more than two sources, custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getKey"},{"type":"text","value":", or\noptimistic mutations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both oracles use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mockSyncCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" as a controlled provider. Their\nclaims are limited to how the Collection and compiler handle the sync\ntransactions that provider supplies; neither claims a real adapter's behavior."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"red-and-green-evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RED and GREEN evidence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutants ran on the reviewed tree through the oracle files alone. Each file was\nrestored after each run."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":" returns FALSE for a nullish operand"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WHERE"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure in the pinned snapshot tests and both campaigns (5 tests)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Routing ignores a change's previous value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WHERE"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure in the change pinned tests and both campaigns (4 tests)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Routing continues while stale published rows await reconciliation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WHERE"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure in the restarted-source witness."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Routing treats "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"or"},{"type":"text","value":" operands as conjuncts"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WHERE"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure in the change pinned tests and a campaign (2 tests)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A dropped insert or update is recorded as sent"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WHERE"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Survived: equivalent in this oracle's domain. Routing withholds a dropped row from an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":" subscription before any key is recorded, and unrouted predicates never skip the delete that would expose a stale record."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same sent-key mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-subscription.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" page-offset witness"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Survived the pre-review "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":" witness. After the review added an unrouted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"or"},{"type":"text","value":" predicate, the two cases beside a matching change fail by assertion (offset 3, expected 2). The unrouted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alone"},{"type":"text","value":" case survives."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result key joins the source keys with a comma"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Join"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key-count invariant error or wrong-row assertion in both pinned delimiter and number histories and both campaigns (4 tests)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result key uses plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"JSON.stringify"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Join"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wrong-row assertion in the pinned infinity history and both campaigns (3 tests)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Index compares source-key prefixes with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"==="}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Join"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key-count invariant error in the pinned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" history and the fixed campaign (2 tests)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same prefix comparison"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Index refinement oracle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure in three pinned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" histories and both campaigns; the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" control passes."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The plain-JSON mutant is the pre-review implementation. The review found that\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"JSON.stringify"},{"type":"text","value":" prints "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":" as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":", the marker for a\nmissing outer side. Adding both infinities to the key domain failed the fixed\nand random campaigns with a full join over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":" on both sides. The repair\nencodes a non-finite number as an object, which no source key can be. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":"\nsource keys failed before key encoding mattered, with the comma encoding as\nwell, because the join index compared source-key prefixes with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"==="},{"type":"text","value":", so a\nretracted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":"-keyed row never cancelled. The review fixed that comparison in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db-ivm"},{"type":"text","value":" and added an Index refinement oracle for it; the join oracle\nnow includes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" keys and a pinned history in which a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":"-keyed pair leaves\nand re-forms."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After the repairs, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"test:oracles"},{"type":"text","value":" at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_RUNS_MULTIPLIER=10"},{"type":"text","value":"\npassed 2,869 tests and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" suite passed 7,499 tests. Final\nverification receipts belong in the pull request."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-predicate-publication-oracle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WHERE predicate publication oracle"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 Contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The evaluator contract in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" states the operand rules. The opening prose lists the omissions and their owners."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 Independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedTruth"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedVisible"},{"type":"text","value":" evaluate plain objects without production comparison, normalization, prefilter, routing, or virtual-field helpers."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The contract, model, history grammar, production driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 Generated-history controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass after repair. Reconstruction: the pinned snapshot with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq($synced, false)"},{"type":"text","value":" was outside the grammar because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" was not a literal; the review added it, and every pinned history is now in the grammar. Ablation: removing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"not"},{"type":"text","value":" loses the nullish-comparison history, removing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"or"},{"type":"text","value":" loses the routing history, removing multi-operation transactions loses retraction inside one batch, removing insert reuse loses reinsertion, and removing the index axis loses the index path. Range: depth three, at most six rows, six transactions of three operations, and marginal values "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":", a valid Date, the normalization prefix, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":", and a missing field. Exclusion: an update to an equivalent value and a second update to one key in one transaction are removed from the history."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005 Production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Public builder functions, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribeChanges"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"currentStateAsChanges"},{"type":"text","value":" run against a real Collection. Each consumer's exact key set is compared after the subscriptions attach and after each sync transaction commits."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 Checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The checker test requires the two-valued answer to fail. The mutant table classifies each run."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 Fixed/random replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The fixed seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"44_500_301"},{"type":"text","value":", the unseeded campaign, and the replay entry share one property and budget."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The model keeps a row map across sync transactions. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$synced"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$origin"},{"type":"text","value":" separate a pending optimistic insert from a synced row, and the pinned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq($synced, false)"},{"type":"text","value":" snapshot distinguishes them. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$key"},{"type":"text","value":" always equals the row id; it stays because the grammar reads it as an operand. The touched-key set distinguishes a subscription without initial state from one with it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass after repair. The prose now uses live-query Collection and sync transaction. A subscriber is the callback of one subscription; a consumer is the oracle's label for one observed endpoint."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass after repair. Cleanup ran in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":", so a cleanup failure replaced the check failure. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withOracleCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":" now runs every cleanup step and keeps the check failure as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cause"},{"type":"text","value":" of an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregateError"},{"type":"text","value":" when cleanup also fails."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011 Independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. No review has named a fault that the Kleene model and production could share."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 Review evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record. The coverage map links it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013 Reusable boundary law"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. FALSE-for-UNKNOWN is rejected by the negated nullish snapshot. Ignoring the previous value is rejected by generated change histories. Treating "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"or"},{"type":"text","value":" operands as conjuncts is rejected by the pinned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"or"},{"type":"text","value":" change history. Routing past stale rows is rejected by the restarted-source witness."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. The claim is limited to the controlled provider's sync transactions."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"joined-result-key-oracle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Joined result key oracle"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 Contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass after repair. The prose now cites the live-query guide: joins behave like SQL joins, and a join result has a composite key of the parent keys. The guide does not fix the key format."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 Independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedPairs"},{"type":"text","value":" is a nested loop over plain arrays and does not import the compiler or its key encoding."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The contract, model, history grammar, production driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 Generated-history controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass after repair. Reconstruction: the pinned number history used right key "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":", which was outside the key domain; it now uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c"},{"type":"text","value":", and all three pinned histories are in the grammar. Ablation: removing delimiter strings, number and string twins, or infinities loses a collision class; removing full joins loses unmatched rows on the right; removing synced changes loses unmatched rows created by a group move. Range: at most five rows a side, groups 0 through 2, and three changes. Exclusion: keys are unique within one side, and a change that keeps a row's group is skipped."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005 Production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createLiveQueryCollection"},{"type":"text","value":" compiles a real join. Published pairs and the key count are compared after preload and after each synced change."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 Checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The comma and plain-JSON mutants fail, as classified above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 Fixed/random replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The fixed seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"44_501_962"},{"type":"text","value":", the unseeded campaign, and the replay entry share one property and budget."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. The model recomputes the pairs from the current rows."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. A pair is one published row of the live-query Collection, described by its two source keys."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass after repair, through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withOracleCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011 Independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. No shared-fault hypothesis has been named."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 Review evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record. The coverage map links it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013 Reusable boundary law"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The law is that distinct pairs have distinct keys and a retracted pair cancels. The comma encoding is rejected by the delimiter and number pinned histories, plain JSON by the infinity pinned history, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"==="},{"type":"text","value":" prefixes by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" pinned history."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. The claim is limited to how the compiler keys rows from the controlled provider."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"open-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open work"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sent-key mutant survives the unrouted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alone"},{"type":"text","value":" page-offset case."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generated cleanup and restart histories for filtered subscriptions remain\nwith the lifecycle publication owner, as the coverage map records."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"where-predicate-publication-and-joined-result-key-oracle-review","text":"WHERE predicate publication and joined result key oracle review","level":1},{"id":"reviewed-state-and-claim","text":"Reviewed state and claim","level":2},{"id":"red-and-green-evidence","text":"RED and GREEN evidence","level":2},{"id":"where-predicate-publication-oracle","text":"WHERE predicate publication oracle","level":2},{"id":"joined-result-key-oracle","text":"Joined result key oracle","level":2},{"id":"open-work","text":"Open work","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# WHERE predicate publication and joined result key oracle review\n\n## Reviewed state and claim\n\nBase: `49dc79d4`, the head of pull request #1956 before this review. This\nrecord reviews the Git tree that contains it. The final commit or pull request\nidentifies that immutable tree. The review applied the ORC-001 through ORC-014\nrequirements of the oracle guide revision that adds ORC-013 and ORC-014.\n\nThe WHERE predicate publication oracle claims that a filtered live-query\nCollection, direct subscriptions with and without initial state, peer\nsubscriptions routed by one `eq` field, and `currentStateAsChanges` publish\nexactly the rows whose predicate is TRUE under SQL three-valued logic. The\nclaim covers the grammar in the oracle's opening prose. It does not cover\ncomparison operators other than `eq`, joins, ordering, optimistic updates or\ndeletes, truncate, failed replay, or generated cleanup and restart histories.\n\nThe joined result key oracle claims that an inner, left, or full two-source\njoin publishes one row per joined pair and that distinct pairs never share a\nresult key, for string keys, numeric keys, both infinities, and `NaN`. It does\nnot cover joins over subqueries, more than two sources, custom `getKey`, or\noptimistic mutations.\n\nBoth oracles use `mockSyncCollectionOptions` as a controlled provider. Their\nclaims are limited to how the Collection and compiler handle the sync\ntransactions that provider supplies; neither claims a real adapter's behavior.\n\n## RED and GREEN evidence\n\nMutants ran on the reviewed tree through the oracle files alone. Each file was\nrestored after each run.\n\n| Mutant | Oracle | Outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `eq` returns FALSE for a nullish operand | WHERE | Assertion failure in the pinned snapshot tests and both campaigns (5 tests). |\n| Routing ignores a change's previous value | WHERE | Assertion failure in the change pinned tests and both campaigns (4 tests). |\n| Routing continues while stale published rows await reconciliation | WHERE | Assertion failure in the restarted-source witness. |\n| Routing treats `or` operands as conjuncts | WHERE | Assertion failure in the change pinned tests and a campaign (2 tests). |\n| A dropped insert or update is recorded as sent | WHERE | Survived: equivalent in this oracle's domain. Routing withholds a dropped row from an `eq` subscription before any key is recorded, and unrouted predicates never skip the delete that would expose a stale record. |\n| The same sent-key mutant | `collection-subscription.test.ts` page-offset witness | Survived the pre-review `eq` witness. After the review added an unrouted `or` predicate, the two cases beside a matching change fail by assertion (offset 3, expected 2). The unrouted `alone` case survives. |\n| Result key joins the source keys with a comma | Join | Key-count invariant error or wrong-row assertion in both pinned delimiter and number histories and both campaigns (4 tests). |\n| Result key uses plain `JSON.stringify` | Join | Wrong-row assertion in the pinned infinity history and both campaigns (3 tests). |\n| Index compares source-key prefixes with `===` | Join | Key-count invariant error in the pinned `NaN` history and the fixed campaign (2 tests). |\n| The same prefix comparison | Index refinement oracle | Assertion failure in three pinned `NaN` histories and both campaigns; the `-0` control passes. |\n\nThe plain-JSON mutant is the pre-review implementation. The review found that\n`JSON.stringify` prints `Infinity` and `-Infinity` as `null`, the marker for a\nmissing outer side. Adding both infinities to the key domain failed the fixed\nand random campaigns with a full join over `-Infinity` on both sides. The repair\nencodes a non-finite number as an object, which no source key can be. `NaN`\nsource keys failed before key encoding mattered, with the comma encoding as\nwell, because the join index compared source-key prefixes with `===`, so a\nretracted `NaN`-keyed row never cancelled. The review fixed that comparison in\n`@tanstack/db-ivm` and added an Index refinement oracle for it; the join oracle\nnow includes `NaN` keys and a pinned history in which a `NaN`-keyed pair leaves\nand re-forms.\n\nAfter the repairs, `test:oracles` at `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_RUNS_MULTIPLIER=10`\npassed 2,869 tests and the `@tanstack/db` suite passed 7,499 tests. Final\nverification receipts belong in the pull request.\n\n## WHERE predicate publication oracle\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | Pass. The evaluator contract in `src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts` states the operand rules. The opening prose lists the omissions and their owners. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | Pass. `expectedTruth` and `expectedVisible` evaluate plain objects without production comparison, normalization, prefilter, routing, or virtual-field helpers. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The contract, model, history grammar, production driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Pass after repair. Reconstruction: the pinned snapshot with `eq($synced, false)` was outside the grammar because `false` was not a literal; the review added it, and every pinned history is now in the grammar. Ablation: removing `not` loses the nullish-comparison history, removing `or` loses the routing history, removing multi-operation transactions loses retraction inside one batch, removing insert reuse loses reinsertion, and removing the index axis loses the index path. Range: depth three, at most six rows, six transactions of three operations, and marginal values `NaN`, a valid Date, the normalization prefix, `null`, and a missing field. Exclusion: an update to an equivalent value and a second update to one key in one transaction are removed from the history. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | Pass. Public builder functions, `subscribeChanges`, and `currentStateAsChanges` run against a real Collection. Each consumer's exact key set is compared after the subscriptions attach and after each sync transaction commits. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | Pass. The checker test requires the two-valued answer to fail. The mutant table classifies each run. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Pass. The fixed seed `44_500_301`, the unseeded campaign, and the replay entry share one property and budget. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Pass. The model keeps a row map across sync transactions. `$synced` and `$origin` separate a pending optimistic insert from a synced row, and the pinned `eq($synced, false)` snapshot distinguishes them. `$key` always equals the row id; it stays because the grammar reads it as an operand. The touched-key set distinguishes a subscription without initial state from one with it. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | Pass after repair. The prose now uses live-query Collection and sync transaction. A subscriber is the callback of one subscription; a consumer is the oracle's label for one observed endpoint. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass after repair. Cleanup ran in `finally`, so a cleanup failure replaced the check failure. `withOracleCleanup` now runs every cleanup step and keeps the check failure as the `cause` of an `AggregateError` when cleanup also fails. |\n| ORC-011 Independent second formulation | Not triggered. No review has named a fault that the Kleene model and production could share. |\n| ORC-012 Review evidence | This record. The coverage map links it. |\n| ORC-013 Reusable boundary law | Pass. FALSE-for-UNKNOWN is rejected by the negated nullish snapshot. Ignoring the previous value is rejected by generated change histories. Treating `or` operands as conjuncts is rejected by the pinned `or` change history. Routing past stale rows is rejected by the restarted-source witness. |\n| ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff | Not triggered. The claim is limited to the controlled provider's sync transactions. |\n\n## Joined result key oracle\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | Pass after repair. The prose now cites the live-query guide: joins behave like SQL joins, and a join result has a composite key of the parent keys. The guide does not fix the key format. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | Pass. `expectedPairs` is a nested loop over plain arrays and does not import the compiler or its key encoding. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The contract, model, history grammar, production driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Pass after repair. Reconstruction: the pinned number history used right key `x`, which was outside the key domain; it now uses `c`, and all three pinned histories are in the grammar. Ablation: removing delimiter strings, number and string twins, or infinities loses a collision class; removing full joins loses unmatched rows on the right; removing synced changes loses unmatched rows created by a group move. Range: at most five rows a side, groups 0 through 2, and three changes. Exclusion: keys are unique within one side, and a change that keeps a row's group is skipped. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | Pass. `createLiveQueryCollection` compiles a real join. Published pairs and the key count are compared after preload and after each synced change. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | Pass. The comma and plain-JSON mutants fail, as classified above. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Pass. The fixed seed `44_501_962`, the unseeded campaign, and the replay entry share one property and budget. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Not triggered. The model recomputes the pairs from the current rows. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | Pass. A pair is one published row of the live-query Collection, described by its two source keys. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass after repair, through `withOracleCleanup`. |\n| ORC-011 Independent second formulation | Not triggered. No shared-fault hypothesis has been named. |\n| ORC-012 Review evidence | This record. The coverage map links it. |\n| ORC-013 Reusable boundary law | Pass. The law is that distinct pairs have distinct keys and a retracted pair cancels. The comma encoding is rejected by the delimiter and number pinned histories, plain JSON by the infinity pinned history, and `===` prefixes by the `NaN` pinned history. |\n| ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff | Not triggered. The claim is limited to how the compiler keys rows from the controlled provider. |\n\n## Open work\n\n- The sent-key mutant survives the unrouted `alone` page-offset case.\n- Generated cleanup and restart histories for filtered subscriptions remain\n  with the lifecycle publication owner, as the coverage map records.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# WHERE predicate publication and joined result key oracle review\n\n## Reviewed state and claim\n\nBase: `49dc79d4`, the head of pull request #1956 before this review. This\nrecord reviews the Git tree that contains it. The final commit or pull request\nidentifies that immutable tree. The review applied the ORC-001 through ORC-014\nrequirements of the oracle guide revision that adds ORC-013 and ORC-014.\n\nThe WHERE predicate publication oracle claims that a filtered live-query\nCollection, direct subscriptions with and without initial state, peer\nsubscriptions routed by one `eq` field, and `currentStateAsChanges` publish\nexactly the rows whose predicate is TRUE under SQL three-valued logic. The\nclaim covers the grammar in the oracle's opening prose. It does not cover\ncomparison operators other than `eq`, joins, ordering, optimistic updates or\ndeletes, truncate, failed replay, or generated cleanup and restart histories.\n\nThe joined result key oracle claims that an inner, left, or full two-source\njoin publishes one row per joined pair and that distinct pairs never share a\nresult key, for string keys, numeric keys, both infinities, and `NaN`. It does\nnot cover joins over subqueries, more than two sources, custom `getKey`, or\noptimistic mutations.\n\nBoth oracles use `mockSyncCollectionOptions` as a controlled provider. Their\nclaims are limited to how the Collection and compiler handle the sync\ntransactions that provider supplies; neither claims a real adapter's behavior.\n\n## RED and GREEN evidence\n\nMutants ran on the reviewed tree through the oracle files alone. Each file was\nrestored after each run.\n\n| Mutant | Oracle | Outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `eq` returns FALSE for a nullish operand | WHERE | Assertion failure in the pinned snapshot tests and both campaigns (5 tests). |\n| Routing ignores a change's previous value | WHERE | Assertion failure in the change pinned tests and both campaigns (4 tests). |\n| Routing continues while stale published rows await reconciliation | WHERE | Assertion failure in the restarted-source witness. |\n| Routing treats `or` operands as conjuncts | WHERE | Assertion failure in the change pinned tests and a campaign (2 tests). |\n| A dropped insert or update is recorded as sent | WHERE | Survived: equivalent in this oracle's domain. Routing withholds a dropped row from an `eq` subscription before any key is recorded, and unrouted predicates never skip the delete that would expose a stale record. |\n| The same sent-key mutant | `collection-subscription.test.ts` page-offset witness | Survived the pre-review `eq` witness. After the review added an unrouted `or` predicate, the two cases beside a matching change fail by assertion (offset 3, expected 2). The unrouted `alone` case survives. |\n| Result key joins the source keys with a comma | Join | Key-count invariant error or wrong-row assertion in both pinned delimiter and number histories and both campaigns (4 tests). |\n| Result key uses plain `JSON.stringify` | Join | Wrong-row assertion in the pinned infinity history and both campaigns (3 tests). |\n| Index compares source-key prefixes with `===` | Join | Key-count invariant error in the pinned `NaN` history and the fixed campaign (2 tests). |\n| The same prefix comparison | Index refinement oracle | Assertion failure in three pinned `NaN` histories and both campaigns; the `-0` control passes. |\n\nThe plain-JSON mutant is the pre-review implementation. The review found that\n`JSON.stringify` prints `Infinity` and `-Infinity` as `null`, the marker for a\nmissing outer side. Adding both infinities to the key domain failed the fixed\nand random campaigns with a full join over `-Infinity` on both sides. The repair\nencodes a non-finite number as an object, which no source key can be. `NaN`\nsource keys failed before key encoding mattered, with the comma encoding as\nwell, because the join index compared source-key prefixes with `===`, so a\nretracted `NaN`-keyed row never cancelled. The review fixed that comparison in\n`@tanstack/db-ivm` and added an Index refinement oracle for it; the join oracle\nnow includes `NaN` keys and a pinned history in which a `NaN`-keyed pair leaves\nand re-forms.\n\nAfter the repairs, `test:oracles` at `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_RUNS_MULTIPLIER=10`\npassed 2,869 tests and the `@tanstack/db` suite passed 7,499 tests. Final\nverification receipts belong in the pull request.\n\n## WHERE predicate publication oracle\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | Pass. The evaluator contract in `src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts` states the operand rules. The opening prose lists the omissions and their owners. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | Pass. `expectedTruth` and `expectedVisible` evaluate plain objects without production comparison, normalization, prefilter, routing, or virtual-field helpers. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The contract, model, history grammar, production driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Pass after repair. Reconstruction: the pinned snapshot with `eq($synced, false)` was outside the grammar because `false` was not a literal; the review added it, and every pinned history is now in the grammar. Ablation: removing `not` loses the nullish-comparison history, removing `or` loses the routing history, removing multi-operation transactions loses retraction inside one batch, removing insert reuse loses reinsertion, and removing the index axis loses the index path. Range: depth three, at most six rows, six transactions of three operations, and marginal values `NaN`, a valid Date, the normalization prefix, `null`, and a missing field. Exclusion: an update to an equivalent value and a second update to one key in one transaction are removed from the history. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | Pass. Public builder functions, `subscribeChanges`, and `currentStateAsChanges` run against a real Collection. Each consumer's exact key set is compared after the subscriptions attach and after each sync transaction commits. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | Pass. The checker test requires the two-valued answer to fail. The mutant table classifies each run. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Pass. The fixed seed `44_500_301`, the unseeded campaign, and the replay entry share one property and budget. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Pass. The model keeps a row map across sync transactions. `$synced` and `$origin` separate a pending optimistic insert from a synced row, and the pinned `eq($synced, false)` snapshot distinguishes them. `$key` always equals the row id; it stays because the grammar reads it as an operand. The touched-key set distinguishes a subscription without initial state from one with it. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | Pass after repair. The prose now uses live-query Collection and sync transaction. A subscriber is the callback of one subscription; a consumer is the oracle's label for one observed endpoint. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass after repair. Cleanup ran in `finally`, so a cleanup failure replaced the check failure. `withOracleCleanup` now runs every cleanup step and keeps the check failure as the `cause` of an `AggregateError` when cleanup also fails. |\n| ORC-011 Independent second formulation | Not triggered. No review has named a fault that the Kleene model and production could share. |\n| ORC-012 Review evidence | This record. The coverage map links it. |\n| ORC-013 Reusable boundary law | Pass. FALSE-for-UNKNOWN is rejected by the negated nullish snapshot. Ignoring the previous value is rejected by generated change histories. Treating `or` operands as conjuncts is rejected by the pinned `or` change history. Routing past stale rows is rejected by the restarted-source witness. |\n| ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff | Not triggered. The claim is limited to the controlled provider's sync transactions. |\n\n## Joined result key oracle\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 Contract authority and limits | Pass after repair. The prose now cites the live-query guide: joins behave like SQL joins, and a join result has a composite key of the parent keys. The guide does not fix the key format. |\n| ORC-002 Independent judgment | Pass. `expectedPairs` is a nested loop over plain arrays and does not import the compiler or its key encoding. |\n| ORC-003 Distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The contract, model, history grammar, production driver, and refinement check are separate marked sections. |\n| ORC-004 Generated-history controls | Pass after repair. Reconstruction: the pinned number history used right key `x`, which was outside the key domain; it now uses `c`, and all three pinned histories are in the grammar. Ablation: removing delimiter strings, number and string twins, or infinities loses a collision class; removing full joins loses unmatched rows on the right; removing synced changes loses unmatched rows created by a group move. Range: at most five rows a side, groups 0 through 2, and three changes. Exclusion: keys are unique within one side, and a change that keeps a row's group is skipped. |\n| ORC-005 Production path and observation | Pass. `createLiveQueryCollection` compiles a real join. Published pairs and the key count are compared after preload and after each synced change. |\n| ORC-006 Checker calibration | Pass. The comma and plain-JSON mutants fail, as classified above. |\n| ORC-007 Fixed/random replay | Pass. The fixed seed `44_501_962`, the unseeded campaign, and the replay entry share one property and budget. |\n| ORC-008 Stateful-model minimality | Not triggered. The model recomputes the pairs from the current rows. |\n| ORC-009 Vocabulary mapping | Pass. A pair is one published row of the live-query Collection, described by its two source keys. |\n| ORC-010 Failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass after repair, through `withOracleCleanup`. |\n| ORC-011 Independent second formulation | Not triggered. No shared-fault hypothesis has been named. |\n| ORC-012 Review evidence | This record. The coverage map links it. |\n| ORC-013 Reusable boundary law | Pass. The law is that distinct pairs have distinct keys and a retracted pair cancels. The comma encoding is rejected by the delimiter and number pinned histories, plain JSON by the infinity pinned history, and `===` prefixes by the `NaN` pinned history. |\n| ORC-014 Controlled-premise handoff | Not triggered. The claim is limited to how the compiler keys rows from the controlled provider. |\n\n## Open work\n\n- The sent-key mutant survives the unrouted `alone` page-offset case.\n- Generated cleanup and restart histories for filtered subscriptions remain\n  with the lifecycle publication owner, as the coverage map records.\n"}
